About_Distribution_land:'Distribution of vested land',
Body_Distribution_land:'<center><b><ins>A Note on Nijo Griha Nijo Bhumi Prakalpa</ins></b></center> <br/> The scheme called Nijo Griha Nijo Bhumi Prakalpa was started on 18th October 2011. The scheme is aimed at providing all landless and homeless families 3 cottahs (approx 5 decimal) of land per family by issuing patta on vested land or through direct purchase so that there will be no landless and homeless family in the rural Bengal. Priority is however given for available vested land for the Scheme and purchase of land should be taken as a last resort. It has been provided in the Scheme so framed that the beneficiary families, by using their family labour and with support from other governmental Schemes, construct their own house on the land and generate additional income. Several such families would be given land in one single cluster plot to facilitate both overall economic and social benefits. This Scheme seeks active linkage and convergence with important and popular developmental schemes of the State and Central government meant for rural households. Indira Abas Yojana( IAY), total Sanitation Campaign(TSC), Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme ( MGNREGS), Rajiv Gandhi Vidyutayan Yojana(RGVY), National Horticulture Mission etc under the Panchayats & Rural Development Department, Agriculture Department, Backward Classes Welfare Department, Minority Welfare & Development & Madrasha Department, Housing Department, Self Help Group & Self Employment Department of the State government are some of them. The District Magistrate & Collector ensures participation of Line Departments and Bankers including National Bank for Agriculture & Rural Development (NABARD) for necessary convergence. <br/>&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp A total of 2,20,992 beneficiaries has already received NGNB patta till January 2018 since launching of the programme. Beneficiaries on vested land and raiyati land are almost covered. Settlement of beneficiaries on other departments&apos; land is under process.  The vision and future plan for NGNB for the coming years would be :<br/><br/> &nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp 1) All NGNB beneficiaries will receive RORs who have already received NGNB patta documents. <br/> &nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp&nbsp 2) Convergence of services in collaberation with other government departments for the NGNB families for improving livelihood by suitably using their patta land. <br/><br/><br/> <b><ins>Distribution of available vested land for agricultural purpose.</ins></b><br/> Vested land is distributed among the eligible beneficiaries for agricultural purpose u/s 49(1) of W.B.L.R Act, 1955. Family owns no land or owns less than one acre or 0.4048 hectare of land will be eligible for such purpose. The maximum area of land that may be distributed to landless person is one acre. In case of a bargadar, half of the land cultivated by him as bargadar is taken into account. Out of 27,31,156 acres of vested land , 11,30,319 acres of land has been distributed as agricultural , homestead and NGNB patta.',

